ood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1.\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186175687 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}} \\\\\n&= (θ^{x_{1}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{1}}) \\cdot (θ^{x_{2}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{2}})⋯(θ^{x_{n}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{n}}) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{∑(1 - x_{i})} \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{n - ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= θ^{S}(1 - θ)^{n - S}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i=7\n$$\n\nand $n=10$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= θ^{7}(1 - θ)^{10 - 7} \\\\\n&= θ^{7}(1 - θ)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 false] [17592186175687 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S=\\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i=7$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{10-7} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186175708 1071 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(4,\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 22$.\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C$.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n$f(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{n-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1,2,\\ldots, n$." 13194139534771 17592186045417 false] [17592186175708 1071 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(4,\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 22.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{n-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1,2,\\ldots, n.\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186175708 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 12$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x; θ) = (\\frac{4}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{4 - x}, & x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{4}{x_{i}})θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{i}} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4}{x_{1}})θ^{x_{1}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{1}}) \\cdot ((\\frac{4}{x_{2}})θ^{x_{2}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{2}})⋯((\\frac{4}{x_{n}})θ^{x_{n}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{n}}) \\\\\n&= Cθ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{∑(4 - x_{i})} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{4n - ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{S}(1 - θ)^{4n - S}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\prod\\limits_{i=1}^n {4 \\choose x_i}\n$$\n\nis a constant and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i = 22\n$$\n\nand $n = 12$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= Cθ^{22}(1 - θ)^{4(12) - 22} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{22}(1 - θ)^{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 false] [17592186175708 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 12$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (4-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,4n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{4n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C = \\prod\\limits_{i=1}^n  {4 \\choose x_i}$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i.$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i = 22$ and $n = 12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{4(12)-22} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186175709 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0, 2, 3, 1, 1$." 13194139534771 17592186045417 false] [17592186175709 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0, 2, 3, 1, 1.$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186175709 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\\,\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)\\frac{θ^{x_{i}}e^{- θ}}{x_{i}!} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{θ^{x_{1}}e^{- θ}}{x_{1}!}) \\cdot (\\frac{θ^{x_{2}}e^{- θ}}{x_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{θ^{x_{n}}e^{- θ}}{x_{n}!}) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}} \\cdot e^{- ∑θ} \\cdot ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(1)/((x_{i})!) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}} \\cdot e^{-nθ} \\cdot (1)/(∏_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i})!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{C}θ^{S}e^{-nθ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\prod\\limits_{i=1}^{n} (x_i)\n$$\n\nis a constant and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 0 + 2 + 3 + 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 7 \\\\\nC &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i})! \\\\\n&= 0! \\cdot 2! \\cdot 3! \\cdot 1! \\cdot 1! \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= \\frac{1}{12}θ^{7}e^{-5θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 false] [17592186175709 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C=\\prod\\limits_{i=1}^{n} (x_i)!$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+2+3+1+1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,{\\theta }^{7}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186176040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435595 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186176040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435597 1