nown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=4,$ and $n=17.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186435597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435592 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186435597 1069 "q-159812" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186435597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186435597 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=7.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" 13194139534771 17592186045417 true] [17592186435597 1072 #uuid "ae249912-4f99-5805-8937-8f946d